About NAT:

- NAT is designed to overcome IPV4 shortages
- Also provides some security benefits
- Translate Private IPV4 addresses to Public
- Static NAT - 1 private to 1 (fixed) public address (IGW)
- Dynamic NAT - 1 private to 1st available Public
- Port Address Translation (PAT) - many private to 1 public (NATGW)
- IPV4 makes no sense for IPV6

## Static Network Address Translation

The route (NAT Device) maintians a NAT table, it maps PrivateIP:Public IP (1:1) 

1. It generates a source ip in order to communicate with Netflix which is the source IP. Packets are generated as normal with a private SOURCE (SRC) IP, and an external DESTINATION (DST) IP.
2. if a Public IP has been allocated to the Private IP the source address of the packet is translated as it passes through the NAT device. src IP: 52.95.36.68 dst IP: Netflix
3. API client communicate swiht CAT API. PAckets arrvie with a SRC of 52.95.36.68
4. CatAPI WebService IP: 1.3.3.7
5. SRC IP: CAT API DST IP: 52.95.36.68
6. SRC IP: CATAPI DST IP: 10.0.0.10  Inbound packet are checked, if a matching entry is found in NAT table, the DST Public IP is translated to the matching Private IP

In AWS this is how Internet Gateway (IGW) functions 

## Dynamic Network Address Translation

The router (NAT Device) this maintain a NAT table, it maps PrivateIP : PublicIP. Public IP allocations are temporary allocation from Public IP Pool. 

1. SRC IP: 10.0.0.10 DST IP: 1.3.3.7 

Public IP allocations are temporary and multiple devices can use the same allocation over time as long as there is no overlap

Only one Private IP will be mapped to 52.95.36.66 at any time. It’s still 1:1 for duration of the allocation 

If the public IP is exhausted external access can fail.

1. 52.95.36.67 10.0.0.10 is dynamically allocated 52.95.36.67 

## Port Address Translation (PAT)

In AWS this is how the NATGateway (NATGW) functions - a (MANY:1) (PRIVATE IP:PUBLICIP) Architecture

The NAT device records the Source (Private) IP and Source Port. this replaces the osurce IP with the single Public IP and a public source port allocated from a pool which allows IP overloading (many to one) 

NAT Public IP: 52.95.36.67

1. SRC IP: 10.0.0.42 SRC PORT: 32768 SRC IP: 1.3.3.7 SRC PORT: 443
2. SRC IP: 52.95.36.67 SRC PORT: 1337 (New src port is given and recorded on NAT table)     SRC IP: 1.3.3.7 SRC PORT: 443
3. And so on 

NAT Table is also saved to show what IP is which what port

Return traffic has tcp/443 and 1.3.3.7 as the source and the Public IP of the NAT device and the destination port

Public Port and Public IP are translated to Private Port and Private IP
